cff-version: 1.2.0
title: tokenpace
message: >-
  If you use these measurements, or the reading-speed baseline derived from
  them, please cite this repository.
type: software

# On the licence field: CFF allows a list, but the schema reads a list as OR,
# which would be a lie here — this project's parts are licensed differently and
# all of them apply at once. So this names the licence of the thing the file
# exists for: the measurements, CC BY 4.0, the one that asks for attribution.
# The code is MIT and the sample texts are CC0. See LICENSES.md.
license: CC-BY-4.0

authors:
  - family-names: Yang
    given-names: Jiwoong
    alias: woongstardev

abstract: >-
  Tokens per second is the unit benchmarks report; characters per second is what
  people read. tokenpace measures the conversion between them — characters per
  token for six tokenizers (o200k_base, cl100k_base, Llama 3.1, Qwen3, Gemma 3,
  Mistral Small 3) across English, Korean, Japanese and Chinese, on 3,000
  parallel sentence pairs per language from TED2020 — and uses it to express
  human reading speed in tok/s. That figure lands near 5 tok/s in both English
  and Korean, which places most current decoding speeds well above it and moves
  the perceptual question to time-to-first-token and answer length — for prose
  that is read rather than skimmed, a scope the project states rather than
  assumes. Every figure
  regenerates from the raw corpora with a committed script; a weekly CI job
  re-derives them and fails if the result differs.

keywords:
  - tokenization
  - tokens per second
  - reading speed
  - large language models
  - Korean
  - visualization

repository-code: https://github.com/woongstardev/tokenpace
url: https://tokenpace.woongstar.com/

# The concept DOI, not the version DOI. It always resolves to the most recent
# archived release, which is what someone citing "tokenpace" means — the
# per-version DOI for v1.0.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.22265788, and each later release
# gets its own. Minted by Zenodo from the GitHub release; this file is what it
# read to get the author, title and licence right.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22265787
version: 1.0.0
date-released: '2026-09-03'

# The sources the measurements rest on. The reading-speed baseline is only as
# good as these two, and both are cited on the site as well as here.
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      How many words do we read per minute? A review and meta-analysis of
      reading rate
    authors:
      - family-names: Brysbaert
        given-names: Marc
    journal: Journal of Memory and Language
    volume: 109
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1016/j.jml.2019.104047
    notes: >-
      English baseline: 238 wpm, silent reading of non-fiction, 190 studies,
      18,573 participants.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      한국어 읽기 속도 측정 애플리케이션의 유효성 및 정상인의 읽기 속도에 대한 사전 연구
    authors:
      - family-names: 송
        given-names: 지호
      - family-names: 김
        given-names: 재형
      - family-names: 형
        given-names: 성민
    journal: 대한안과학회지
    volume: 57
    issue: 4
    start: 642
    end: 649
    year: 2016
    doi: 10.3341/jkos.2016.57.4.642
    notes: >-
      Korean baseline: 549.7 characters/min (SD 348.9), n=42, silent reading at
      10 pt. The standard deviation is 63% of the mean, which is why the site
      lets you measure your own speed instead.
  - type: data
    title: TED2020
    authors:
      - name: OPUS
    year: 2020
    url: https://opus.nlpl.eu/TED2020/corpus/version/TED2020
    notes: >-
      The parallel corpus the density figures are measured on. Derived from TED
      talk transcripts under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, so it is downloaded and checksummed
      rather than redistributed here.
